Expand implied roles in linear time and look up each role once


Listing effective role assignments
(keystone/assignment/core.py, `_list_effective_role_assignments`) pays
twice per resulting assignment. `add_implied_roles` keeps the refs it has
already expanded in a list and tests every new implied ref with
`implied_ref in checked_role_refs`, a dict-equality scan of a list that
grows to the size of the result: 4,000 member assignments that each imply
reader take 0.22 s in the published CobaltCore 2026.1 and 2026.2 images,
and 16,000,000 dict comparisons. `_strip_domain_roles` then calls
`role_api.get_role` once per resulting ref to read its `domain_id`.
Without a cache that is two SQL statements per ref, the role row and its
options (`lazy='subquery'`); with a cache it is one role cache read per
ref. The listing backs GET /v3/role_assignments?effective,
GET /v3/users/{user_id}/projects and the project and domain lists of
GET /v3/auth/projects and GET /v3/auth/domains, and `add_implied_roles`
runs inside every token's role resolution. Upstream reported the slow
listing as https://bugs.launchpad.net/keystone/+bug/1700852 and closed it
by caching the result, which every role grant, project change and group
membership change flushes; https://bugs.launchpad.net/keystone/+bug/1920979
reports it again and is still New. CobaltCore creates role assignments
from namespace orders, so their number grows with every tenant
(issue #1459).

`add_implied_roles` now keeps the checked refs as a set of hashable keys,
a frozenset of a ref's items with the nested indirect dict frozen the same
way, so the cycle check is constant time per ref: the 4,000 assignments
take 0.017 s and make no dict comparison. The expansion order, the
duplicates it keeps and the NotImplemented branch are unchanged.

`_strip_domain_roles` calls `get_role` once per distinct role ID of the
refs and keeps the answer for the refs that repeat it; an empty ref list
makes no call. `get_role` reads through the role cache, which a role
grant does not flush, and resolves an ID the way the database does: on a
database with a case-insensitive collation an assignment may carry the
role ID in another letter case than the role row, and each spelling is
looked up as written, as before. A role that does not exist still raises
RoleNotFound. A first version of this patch read the roles with one
`list_roles_from_ids` call, which nothing caches. Token validation
recomputes its roles through `_strip_domain_roles` whenever a role grant
has flushed the computed assignments, and there that call made two SQL
statements where upstream reads the role cache: under the cold-role-cache
profile of CobaltCore's keystone-pressure job the validate p50 was 8.3 %
(2026.1) and 12.8 % (2026.2) above the unpatched image
(https://github.com/C5C3/cobaltcore/actions/runs/39795997952). No schema
migration, option or new method is involved.

No upstream test pins the old cost, and no existing test moves: the
output, its order and the exceptions stay the same. The patch adds eight
tests to `ImpliedRoleTests` in
keystone/tests/unit/assignment/test_backends.py, run by `SqlImpliedRoles`:
test_add_implied_roles_keeps_order_through_a_cycle,
test_add_implied_roles_with_no_refs,
test_add_implied_roles_without_implied_role_support,
test_effective_listing_looks_up_each_role_once,
test_effective_listing_propagates_role_lookup_error,
test_strip_domain_roles_with_no_refs,
test_strip_domain_roles_raises_for_unknown_role and
test_strip_domain_roles_looks_up_each_role_id_once. The two tests that
look up each role once fail on the unpatched tree.

tests/container-images/verify_keystone.sh (test_implied_role_patch_applied)
asserts against the built image, with a mocked role API, that expanding
4,000 refs compares refs fewer times than there are refs, and that
stripping the domain roles calls get_role once per role and never calls
list_roles_from_ids.

Applies to keystone 30.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

